  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` and a new test file, `tests/test_money.py`. All 13 tests pass with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`.\n\n- **Function:** it uses `divmod`, so `split(10, 3)` gives `[4, 3, 3]` and `split(11, 3)` gives `[4, 4, 3]`. A `parts` value below 1 raises `ValueError`.\n- **Tests:** there was no money test file before, so I wrote one in the same unittest style as `test_pricing.py`. It covers an even split, the remainder going to the first elements, the total always equalling `cents`, `parts=1`, and zero or negative `parts` raising `ValueError`.\n- **Negative amounts:** `split(-10, 3)` gives `[-3, -3, -4]`. The total is still right and the extra cents still go to the first elements, but here that leaves the most negative share last. There's a test for this, so it's easy to change if you'd rather negatives come out as `[-4, -3, -3]`.\n\nNothing is committed. Running the tests also created untracked `__pycache__/` folders.\n\nToken Beggar: routed 1 subtask(s) to sonnet",
